title: FirstLoopAaldenburgTree1
---
-> “Hello.” << if $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidHello == false >>
    She does not react. Unless turning to a new page in her notebook is a greeting in her language.
	-> ”Dr. Aaldenburg?”
	    Either she cannot hear you over the sounds of her own writing, or she is too focused on it to pay attention to you.
	    Or she is just ignoring you. Who can say?
        << set $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidHello = true >>
        << jump FirstLoopAaldenburgTree1 >>
-> “Do you know where Dmitriy Yildiz is?” << if $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidDmitriy == false >>
    If she does, she doesn’t tell you. Her writing stays constant, completely uninterrupted by your question.
	-> “Big guy, black hair, was setting things up around here?”
	    No reply. You have a feeling that she hasn’t seen him anyway. She has no eyes for anything besides her notebook, and the lake.
        << set $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidDmitriy = true >>
        << jump FirstLoopAaldenburgTree1 >>
-> “What are you writing in that notebook?” << if $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidHello == true && $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidDmitriy == true >>
    The woman turns towards you for the first time.
    Aaldenburg: “Leave me alone. I am occupied.”
    Then she turns back to her notebook and resumes work.
    You might have to wait until after the parade if you want to know who she is, from the looks of it.
-> “I’ve done a technical study too. Just graduated.” << if $TechMajor == true && $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidHello == true && $FirstLoopAaldenburgSaidDmitriy == true >>
    The woman stops writing for a brief moment. She resumes at a faster pace, as if realising she betrayed the fact she did in fact notice you were present.
    Aaldenburg: Come talk to me at Nauka Square then later.
    Aaldenburg: I am engaged in field work at this moment. I cannot chit-chat.
    You have an inkling that she will not elaborate on what that field work consists of at this moment.
    You’ll have to wait until after the parade if you want to know who she is, then.

